\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Set 3 - A Classical Monetary Model
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Chapter 2 of Gali (2008)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Now, the dominant analytical framework to study monetary policy is the New
 Keynesian (NK) model (presented in Gali, Chapter 3).
 Its elements are: monopolistic competition and price stickiness.
 Before going into the NK model, we will study a classical monetary model
 featuring: perfect competition and fully flexible prices.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

As stressed below, many of the predicitons of that classical economy are
 strongly at odds with empirical evidence.
 That notwithstanding, the analysis of the classical economy provides a
 benchmark that will be useful in subsequent notes when some of its strong
 assumptions are relaxed.
 It also allows for the introduction of some notation, as well as assumptions
 on preferences and techology that are used in the remainder of our discussion.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In this economy, there are households (consume, supply labor); firms (produce,
 hire labor); the central bank (sets nominal interest rates and/or the money
 supply).
 Following much of the recent literature, the baseline classical model developed
 here attaches a very limited role to money.
 This is a cashless economy, so the only explicit role played by money is
 to serve as a unit of account.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Households
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The representative household seeks to maximize the objective function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{0}{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}U(C_{t},N_{t})
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the quantity consumed of the single good, and 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 denotes hours of work or employment.
 (Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $N_{t}$
\end_inset

 can be interpreted as the number of household members employed, assuming
 a large household and ignoring integer constraints).
 The period utility 
\begin_inset Formula $U(C_{t},N_{t})$
\end_inset

 is assumed to be continuous and twice differentiable, with
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
U_{c,t}\equiv\frac{\partial U(C_{t},N_{t})}{\partial C_{t}}>0,\ U_{cc,t}\equiv\frac{\partial^{2}U(C_{t},N_{t})}{\partial C_{t}^{2}}\leq0\\
U_{n,t}\equiv\frac{\partial U(C_{t},N_{t})}{\partial N_{t}}\le0,\ U_{nn,t}\equiv\frac{\partial^{2}U(C_{t},N_{t})}{\partial N_{t}^{2}}\leq0
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

In words, the marginal utility of consumption 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 is assumed to be positive and nonincreasing, while the marginal disutility
 of labor, 
\begin_inset Formula $-Un,t$
\end_inset

, is positive and nondecreasing.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Maximization of (1) is subject to a sequence of flow budget constraints
 given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{t}C_{t}+Q_{t}B_{t}\leq B_{t-1}+W_{t}N_{t}-T_{t};\ \forall\ t=0,1,\dots
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the price of the consumption good.
 
\begin_inset Formula $W_{t}$
\end_inset

 denotes the nominal wage, 
\begin_inset Formula $B_{t}$
\end_inset

 represents the quantity of one-period nominally riskless discount bonds
 purchased in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and maturing in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

.
 Each bond pays one unit of money at maturity and its price is 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{t}$
\end_inset

 represents lump-sum additions or subtractions to period (i.e., lump-sum taxes,
 dividends, etc.), express in nominal terms.
 When solving the problem above, the household is assumed to take as given
 the price of the good, the wage, and the price of bonds.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In addition to (2), it is assume that the household is subject to a solvency
 constraint that prevents it from engaging in Ponzi-type schemes.
 The following constraint:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \lim_{T\rightarrow\infty}}E_{t}\left\{ B_{T}\right\} \geq0;\ \forall\ t
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Optimal Consumption and Labor Supply
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Lagrangian is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=E_{0}\left[{\displaystyle \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}}\beta^{t}\{U(C_{t},N_{t})+\lambda_{t}(-P_{t}C_{t}-Q_{t}B_{t}+B_{t-1}+W_{t}N_{t}-T_{t})\}\right]
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and the first-order conditions are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
U_{c,t}-\lambda_{t}P_{t}=0\iff\lambda_{t}=\frac{U_{c,t}}{P_{t}}\\
U_{n,t}+\lambda_{t}W_{t}=0\\
\lambda_{t}(-Q_{t})+E_{t}\left[\beta\lambda_{t+1}\right]=0
\end{gather}

\end_inset

Combining equations (4) and (5) we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
U_{n,t}+\frac{1}{P_{t}}U_{c,t}W_{t}=0\nonumber \\
-U_{N,t}=U_{c,t}\left(\frac{W_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)
\end{gather}

\end_inset

Combining equations (4) and (6)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{Q_{t}}{P_{t}}U_{c,t}=E_{t}\left[\beta\frac{U_{c,t+1}}{P_{t+1}}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Interpretations 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
(4):
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the marginal utility of nominal income.
 If the household gets an extra dollar, he can purchase 
\begin_inset Formula $(\frac{1}{P_{t}})$
\end_inset

 additional units of the consumption good, while 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 is his marginal utility of consumption.
 Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(\frac{U_{c,t}}{P_{t}}\right)$
\end_inset

 is the marginal benefit of nominal income, measured in utility.
 It is the additional utility the household receives by obtaining one more
 unit of income.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
(7):
\series default
 On the LHS, 
\begin_inset Formula $-U_{n,t}$
\end_inset

 is the opportunity cost of working (i.e., the marginal disutility of labor).
 On the RHS, 
\begin_inset Formula $(\frac{W_{t}}{P_{t}})$
\end_inset

 is the additional units of the consumption good the household can purchase
 if he works an extra hour, while 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 is his marginal utility of consumption.
 Thus, the RHS shows the marginal benefit of working, measured in utility.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
(8):
\series default
 On the LHS, 
\begin_inset Formula $(\frac{Q_{t}}{P_{t}})$
\end_inset

 is the additional units of the consumption good the household can purchase
 if he sells a bond at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, while 
\begin_inset Formula $U_{c,t}$
\end_inset

 is his marginal utility of consumption.

\emph on
 
\emph default
Thus, the LHS is the marginal benefit you get by selling a bond in period
 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

, measured in utility.
 On the RHS, 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{t}\left\{ \beta\frac{U_{c,t+1}}{P_{t+1}}\right\} $
\end_inset

 is the expected discounted marginal benefit of consumption in period 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

, which is the additional utility the household receives if he keeps the
 bond until period 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Assumption on Utility
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Much of what follows, assumes that the period utility takes the form 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
U(C_{t},N_{t})=\frac{C_{t}^{1-\sigma}}{1-\sigma}-\frac{N_{t}^{1+\varphi}}{1+\varphi}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Thus, the consumer's optimality conditions (7) and (8) become
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
N_{t}^{\varphi} & = & C_{t}^{-\sigma}\left(\frac{W_{t}}{P_{t}}\right)\\
Q_{t} & = & E_{t}\left[\beta\left(\frac{C_{t+1}}{C_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\frac{P_{t}}{P_{t+1}}\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Condition (10) can be interpreted as a competitive labor supply schedule,
 determining the quantity of labor supplied as a function of the real wage,
 given the marginal utility of consumption (which under the assumptions
 is a function of consumption only).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Log-Linearization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note, for future reference, that equation (10) can be rewritten in log-linear
 form as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
w_{t}-p_{t}=\sigma c_{t}+\varphi n_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the lower case letters denote the natural logs of the corresponding
 variable (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{t}\equiv\log X_{t}$
\end_inset

).
 We can also obtain a log-linear approximation of (11) around a steady state
 with constant rates of inflation and consumption growth
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
1 & = & E_{t}\left[\beta\left(\frac{C_{t+1}}{C_{t}}\right)^{-\sigma}\frac{P_{t}}{P_{t+1}}\frac{1}{Q_{t}}\right]\\
1 & = & E_{t}\left\{ \exp\left[-\sigma(c_{t+1}-c_{t})-\pi_{t+1}+i_{t}-\rho\right]\right\} 
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi_{t+1}\equiv p_{t+1}-p_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the inflation between 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t+1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{t}\equiv-\log Q_{t}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho\equiv-\log\beta$
\end_inset

 is the discount rate.
 We now construct the first-order Taylor approximation of the RHS around
 the zero inflation steady state, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $(c_{t},c_{t+1},\pi_{t+1},i_{t})=(c,c,0,\rho)$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
1 & = & E_{t}\left\{ 1+(-\sigma)(c_{t+1}-c)+\sigma(c_{t}-c)-\pi_{t+1}+(i_{t}-\rho)\right\} \nonumber \\
0 & = & E_{t}\left\{ -\sigma(c_{t+1}-c_{t})-\pi_{t+1}+i_{t}-\rho\right\} \nonumber \\
c_{t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ c_{t+1}\right\} -\frac{1}{\sigma}\left(i_{t}-E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} -\rho\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Notice that 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{t}$
\end_inset

 corresponds to the log of the gross yield on the one-period bond; henceforth,
 it is referred to as the 
\emph on
nominal interest rate
\emph default
.
 The yield on the one period bond is defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $Q_{t}\equiv(1+\mbox{yield})^{-1}$
\end_inset

.
 Note that 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{t}\equiv-\log Q_{t}=\log(1+\mbox{yield})\approx\mbox{yield}_{t}$
\end_inset

, where the latter approximation will be accurate as long as the nominal
 yield is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

small.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 Similarly, 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

 can be interpreted as the household's discount rate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Firms
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A representative firm is assumed whose technology is described by a production
 function given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Y_{t}=A_{t}N_{t}^{1-\alpha}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{t}$
\end_inset

 represents the level of technology, and 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{t}\equiv\log A_{t}$
\end_inset

 evolves exogenously according to some stochastic process.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Each period the firm maximizes profits
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P_{t}Y_{t}-W_{t}N_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

subject to (11), taking the price and wage as given.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Maximization of (14) subject to (15) yields the optimality condition
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P_{t}(1-\alpha)\frac{Y_{t}}{N_{t}}-W_{t} & = & 0\nonumber \\
(1-\alpha)\frac{Y_{t}}{N_{t}} & = & \frac{W_{t}}{P_{t}}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Thus, optimal labor is obtained by the firm when the marginal product of
 labor (LHS) equals the real wage (RHS).
 Equivalently, the marginal cost 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{W_{t}}{(1-\alpha)A_{t}N_{t}^{-\alpha}}$
\end_inset

 must be equated to the price 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Log-Linearization
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The log-linear aggregate production relationship is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{t}=a_{t}+(1-\alpha)n_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

We can also rewrite the firm's optimality condition in log-linear terms
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\log\left[\frac{W_{t}}{P_{t}}\right] & = & \log\left[(1-\alpha)\frac{Y_{t}}{N_{t}}\right]\nonumber \\
\log W_{t}-\log P_{t} & = & \log\left[(1-\alpha)A_{t}N_{t}^{-\alpha}\right]\nonumber \\
w_{t}-p_{t} & = & a_{t}-\alpha n_{t}+\log(1-\alpha)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Equation (18) can be interpreted as labor demand schedule, mapping the real
 wage into the quantity of labor demanded, give the level of technology.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The baseline model abstracts from aggregate demand components like investment,
 government purchases, or net exports.
 Accordingly, the goods market clearing conditions is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y_{t}=c_{t}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Most importantly, the central bank chooses the nominal interest rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $i_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can summarize the model using the following equations:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\omega_{t}=\sigma c_{t}+\varphi n_{t}\\
c_{t}=E_{t}\left\{ c_{t+1}\right\} -\frac{1}{\sigma}\left(i_{t}-E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} -\rho\right)\\
\omega_{t}=a_{t}-\alpha n_{t}+\log(1-\alpha)\\
y_{t}=a_{t}+(1-\alpha)n_{t}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

This is a system of 4 equations and 4 endogenous variables 
\begin_inset Formula $(n_{t},y_{t},\omega_{t},\pi_{t+1})$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{t}\equiv w_{t}-p_{t}$
\end_inset

 is the real wage at time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First, we combine the optimality conditions of households and firms with
 (19) and the log-linear aggregate production relationship and solve for
 
\begin_inset Formula $n_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\sigma\left[a_{t}+(1-\alpha)n_{t}\right]+\varphi n_{t} & = & a_{t}-\alpha n_{t}+\log(1-\alpha)\nonumber \\
\sigma(1-\alpha)n_{t}+\varphi n_{t}+\alpha n_{t} & = & a_{t}-\sigma a_{t}+\log(1-\alpha)\nonumber \\
\left[\sigma(1-\alpha)+\varphi+\alpha\right]n_{t} & = & (1-\sigma)a_{t}+\log(1-\alpha)\nonumber \\
n_{t} & = & \frac{1-\sigma}{\sigma(1-\alpha)+\varphi+\alpha}a_{t}+\frac{\log(1-\alpha)}{\sigma(1-\alpha)+\varphi+\alpha}\nonumber \\
n_{t} & = & \psi_{na}a_{t}+\nu_{n}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\psi_{na}\equiv\frac{1-\sigma}{\sigma(1-\alpha)+\varphi+\alpha}\ \mbox{and}\ \nu_{n}\equiv\frac{\log(1-\alpha)}{\sigma(1-\alpha)+\varphi+\alpha}
\]

\end_inset

Equation (20) is the equilibrium level of employment as a function of the
 level of technology in the current period.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, we will analyze the firm's production function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{t} & = & a_{t}+(1-\alpha)n_{t}\nonumber \\
 & = & a_{t}+(1-\alpha)\left[\psi_{na}a_{t}+\nu_{n}\right]\nonumber \\
 & = & \left[1+(1-\alpha)\psi_{na}\right]a_{t}+(1-\alpha)\nu_{n}\nonumber \\
 & = & \psi_{ya}a_{t}+\nu_{y}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\psi_{ya}\equiv\frac{1+\varphi}{\sigma(1-\alpha)+\varphi+\alpha}\ \mbox{and}\ \nu_{y}\equiv(1-\alpha)\nu_{n}
\]

\end_inset

Equation (21) is the equilibrium level of output as a function of the level
 of technology in the current period.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Furthermore, given the equilibrium process for output, (13) can be used
 to determine the implied real interest rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{t}\equiv i_{t}-E_{t}\left\{ \pi_{t+1}\right\} $
\end_inset

, as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
y_{t} & = & E_{t}\left\{ y_{t+1}\right\} -\frac{1}{\sigma}(r_{t}-\rho)\nonumber \\
\frac{1}{\sigma}(r_{t}-\rho) & = & E_{t}\left\{ \Delta y_{t+1}\right\} \nonumber \\
r_{t} & = & \rho+\sigma\psi_{ya}E_{t}\left[\Delta a_{t+1}\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta y_{t+1}\equiv y_{t+1}-y_{t}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta a_{t+1}\equiv a_{t+1}-a_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, the equilibrium real wage 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{t}\equiv w_{t}-p_{t}$
\end_inset

 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\omega_{t} & = & a_{t}-\alpha n_{t}+\log(1-\alpha)\\
 & = & a_{t}-\alpha\left(\psi_{na}a_{t}+\nu_{n}\right)+\log(1-\alpha)\\
 & = & \left(1-\alpha\psi_{na}\right)a_{t}-\left[\alpha\nu_{n}+\log(1-\alpha)\right]\\
 & = & \psi_{\omega a}a_{t}+\nu_{\omega}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\psi_{\omega a}\equiv\frac{\sigma+\varphi}{\sigma(1-\alpha)+\varphi+\alpha}\ \mbox{and}\ \nu_{\omega}\equiv\frac{\left[\sigma(1-\alpha)+\varphi\right]\log(1-\alpha)}{\sigma(1-\alpha)+\varphi+\alpha}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Summary
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Notice that the equilibrium dynamics of employment, output, the real interest
 rate, and the real wage rate are determined 
\emph on
independently of monetary policy
\emph default
.
 In other words, monetary policy is 
\emph on
neutral
\emph default
 with respect to those real variables.
 In the simple model, output and employment fluctuate in response to variations
 in technology, which is assumed to be the only real driving force.
 It would be straightforward to introduce other real driving forces like
 variations in government purchases or exogenous shifts in preferences.
 In general, real variables will be affected by all those real shocks in
 equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In particular, output always rises in the face of a productivity increase,
 with the size of the increase being given by 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{ya}>0$
\end_inset

.
 The same is true for the real wage.
 On the other hand, the sign of the employment is ambiguous depending on
 whether 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset

 (which measures the strength of the wealth effect of labor supply) is larger
 or smaller than one.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma<1$
\end_inset

, the substitution effect on labor supply resulting from a higher real wage
 dominates the negative effect caused by a smaller marginal utility of consumpti
on, leading to an increase in employment.
 The converse is true whenever 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma>1$
\end_inset

.
 When the utility of consumption is logarithmic 
\begin_inset Formula $(\sigma=1)$
\end_inset

, employment remains unchanged in the face of technology variations for
 substitution and wealth effects exactly cancel one another.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Next, the response of the real interest rate depends critically on the time
 series properties of technology.
 If the current imporvement in technology is transitory so that 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{t}\left\{ a_{t+1}\right\} <a_{t}$
\end_inset

, then the real rate will go down.
 Otherwise, if technology is expected to keep improving, then 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{t}\left\{ a_{t+1}\right\} >a_{t}$
\end_inset

 and the real rate will increase with a rise in 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{t}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, we consider nominal variables, like inflation or the nominal interest
 rate.
 Not surprisingly, and in contrast with real variables, their equilibrium
 behavior cannot be determined uniquely by real forces.
 Instead, it requires specification of how monetary policy is conducted.
\end_layout

